tests/axir - hand-written `.axir` fixtures.

WHY THEY ARE STILL HAND-WRITTEN, NOW THAT THE COMPILER EMITS BODIES.
`axiom symbols --axir --mir` writes the record header, `sig`, `param`,
`region`, and - since 2026-09-04 - the `blk`, `op` and `term` lines of
every function `self_host/mir.ax`'s `mLowerFn` lowers and `mirVerify`
passes. That corpus is thousands of records deep and
`check-mir-roundtrip.sh` round-trips it, so these files stopped being
the only evidence the body arms of the reader work.

What they still are is the corpus for what the compiler does NOT write.
`body.axir` names blocks `entry` and `loop` and carries opcodes -
`alloc`, `store`, `phi` - that this IR does not have, because the
grammar is a format and not a spelling of one lowering: a reader that
only accepted what today's `mLowerFn` emits would refuse the next one's
output. `lowered.axir` is the other side, and is the emitted shape
verbatim: `bb0` numbering, block parameters on the join, `condbr`,
`trapz`, a `trap` block, `call`, and `br` carrying its argument.

The `.bad` files are the other half: each is one way a file can be
malformed, and the gate asserts the reader REFUSES it. A reader that
accepted them would round-trip a shorter file than it was given, which
is the failure a round-trip check on well-formed input alone cannot
see. `blk-param-without-sigil.bad` and `blk-unlabelled.bad` are the two
the widened `blk` line added: it went from a fixed two atoms to a label
plus a block-parameter list when the body started being written, so
"too few fields" and "a register spelled without its `%`" are both
reachable there now.
